Name: registerVoiceCommand

Type: command

Syntax: registerVoiceCommand <phrases>

Summary:
Registers one or more spoken phrases that will be recognised when the
speech recognition engine is listening. When a phrase is recognised,
a <voiceCommand> message is dispatched to the current card.

Introduced: 1.1.0

OS: mac

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- Register a single command phrase
registerVoiceCommand "open stack"

Example:
-- Register several phrases in one call using a return-delimited list
registerVoiceCommand "open stack" & return & "close stack" & return & "save stack"

Example:
-- Build the list dynamically from an array of commands
put empty into tPhrases
repeat for each element tCmd in tCommands
    put tCmd & return after tPhrases
end repeat
registerVoiceCommand tPhrases

Example:
-- Register and then start listening
registerVoiceCommand "next slide"
registerVoiceCommand "previous slide"
startListening

Parameters:
phrases (string): A single spoken phrase or a return-delimited list of
phrases to register. Each phrase should be natural-sounding text in the
recognition language. Duplicate phrases (case-insensitive) are silently
ignored.

Description:
Use <registerVoiceCommand> to tell the speech recognition engine which
phrases it should watch for. When a registered phrase is recognised while
the engine is listening, a <voiceCommand> message is sent to the current
card of the default stack with the matched phrase as its parameter.

You can call <registerVoiceCommand> multiple times to build up a library
of commands, or pass all phrases at once as a return-delimited list in a
single call — both styles produce identical results.

Registering a phrase that is already registered is a no-op; the existing
registration is not duplicated.

Speech recognition must be started separately with <startListening>.
Registering phrases before calling <startListening> is the recommended
order.

Platform notes:
- macOS: Implemented using SFSpeechRecognizer + AVAudioEngine
  (macOS 10.15+). The user must grant microphone and speech recognition
  permissions. On macOS 13 and later, on-device recognition is used
  automatically (no network required). The registered phrases are passed
  as contextualStrings to bias recognition accuracy toward them.
- Windows: Not yet implemented. Sets the result to a "not supported"
  string.
- Linux: Not yet implemented. Sets the result to a "not supported"
  string.

References: unregisterVoiceCommand (command), unregisterAllVoiceCommands (command),
startListening (command), stopListening (command), setWakeWord (command),
voiceCommand (message), isListening (function)

Tags: speech, voice
